tool-section bytes: bash arm 197, typed arm 587 (+390 bytes, ≈97 extra prompt tokens on every model request of the pass)
model requests: bash arm [2], typed arm [2] — the schema rides on every one of them, which is the honest way to price it

Both arms called tools on these runs: bash arm ['bash'], typed arm ['bd_ready'].

Neither arm is cheaper: the typed arm is *typed* — schema-validated params,
no shell quoting, the refusal path returned as data, and `--tools` gating a
bare bash command cannot have. That is what the control arm was for: it
isolates the tooling win from the engine question, and the engine buys
neither of them.

